public with sharing class ContactPhoneCleanup {
    /**
     * Normalizes the Phone field of the given contacts by stripping every
     * character that is not a digit or a '+'. Contacts without a phone are
     * skipped. All modified contacts are saved in one partial-success update.
     *
     * @param contactIds up to 200 contact Ids
     * @return a map from the Id of each contact that failed to save to its
     *         first error message (empty when everything saved)
     */
    public static Map<Id, String> normalizePhones(List<Id> contactIds) {
        Map<Id, String> failures = new Map<Id, String>();

        if (contactIds == null || contactIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failures;
        }

        List<Contact> contacts = new List<Contact>();
        for (Contact c : [SELECT Id, Phone FROM Contact WHERE Id IN :contactIds]) {
            if (String.isNotBlank(c.Phone)) {
                c.Phone = c.Phone.replaceAll('[^0-9+]', '');
                contacts.add(c);
            }
        }

        if (contacts.isEmpty()) {
            return failures;
        }

        Database.SaveResult[] results = Database.update(contacts, false);
        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            Database.SaveResult sr = results[i];
            if (!sr.isSuccess()) {
                failures.put(contacts[i].Id, sr.getErrors()[0].getMessage());
            }
        }

        return failures;
    }
}
```

Notes on what I kept and why I touched a couple of things:

- **I kept your overall approach**: filter to contacts that have a phone, normalize, one `Database.update(contacts, false)` after the loop, then walk the save results to build the `Id -> first error` map. That's exactly bulk-safe and partial-success friendly for the "up to 200" case.
- **`[^0-9+]` regex**: this removes anything that isn't a digit or `+`, so `+44 (20) 7946-0018` → `+442079460018` as required. (Strictly the dialer wants a *leading* `+`, but your spec said "not a digit or `+`", so I followed that literally rather than guessing at stripping embedded `+` signs.)
- **`String.isNotBlank` instead of just a null check**: a phone that is an empty/whitespace string effectively has no usable number, and this avoids pushing meaningless updates. If you truly want only `!= null`, swap it, but blank-checking is safer.
- **Correlating results by index**: `Database.update` returns results in the same order as the input list, so `contacts[i]` matches `results[i]`. I query `Phone` (and `Id`) explicitly so the field is loaded before I modify it.
- Added a guard for null/empty input so the method is safe to call defensively.

