public with sharing class CasePriorityHandler {

    /**
     * When a case's account has Rating = 'Hot', set the case Priority to 'High'.
     * Every other case keeps the Priority it was created with, including cases
     * with no account.
     *
     * Note: instead of querying the Account inside the loop (which would throw
     * "Too many SOQL queries: 101" when the ERP integration inserts 200 cases),
     * we gather the Account Ids first and run a single query. Same logic, but
     * it is safe for batches of up to 200 cases.
     */
    public static void setPriorityForHotAccounts(List<Case> newCases) {

        // 1. Collect the AccountIds we need to check (skip cases with no account).
        Set<Id> accountIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId != null) {
                accountIds.add(c.AccountId);
            }
        }

        // Nothing to look up - leave every Priority as entered.
        if (accountIds.isEmpty()) {
            return;
        }

        // 2. One query for all the accounts, stored in a Map by Id.
        Map<Id, Account> accountsById = new Map<Id, Account>(
            [SELECT Id, Rating FROM Account WHERE Id IN :accountIds]
        );

        // 3. Loop the cases and bump Priority for the Hot accounts.
        for (Case c : newCases) {
            if (c.AccountId != null) {
                Account acct = accountsById.get(c.AccountId);
                if (acct != null && acct.Rating == 'Hot') {
                    c.Priority = 'High';
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## Deviation note

The ticket says "make the callout right from the trigger." That isn't possible as literally written, for two reasons, so I deviated the minimum necessary:

1. **Synchronous callouts are not allowed from a trigger.** Apex forbids a callout in the same transaction as the trigger's implicit DML, so the work must run asynchronously. I use a `Queueable` (`Database.AllowsCallouts`) enqueued from the trigger, which is the standard pattern and keeps traceability.
2. **"One request per account" + batches of 200 breaks the callout limit.** 200 separate callouts exceed the 100-callouts-per-transaction governor limit. So the Queueable processes up to 100 accounts (one request each) and **chains itself** for the remainder, keeping one request per account as Finance requested.

Everything else matches the spec: one single-element JSON array per account, `null` country when blank, `callout:ERP/api/customers`, no retry/error handling.

